{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to bakers, street sweepers, and insomniacs. Delivery trucks murmur beside the curb while windows brighten one by one, each square of light suggesting a private story. I like this hour because nothing has fully decided what kind of day it will become. The air feels provisional, as if the world were a page waiting for its first sentence.\n\nNear my apartment, a narrow park runs between two busy roads. By noon it is crowded with dogs, bicycles, lunch wrappers, and hurried conversations. Early in the morning, though, it becomes a small republic of birds. Sparrows argue in hedges. Pigeons patrol the paths with comic seriousness. Occasionally a hawk passes overhead, and every lesser bird falls silent at once.\n\nThat sudden silence always catches me. It reveals how much sound I had stopped hearing. Perhaps attention works that way: we notice the world most clearly when something expected disappears. A clock stops ticking. A refrigerator quits humming. A familiar voice leaves the room. Absence draws a sharp outline around what had seemed ordinary.\n\nThis is not necessarily sad. It can be an invitation. When routine loosens its grip, even briefly, we are free to look again. A cracked wall becomes a map; a puddle becomes a mirror; a stranger\u2019s kindness becomes proof that the day is not merely happening around us, but asking for our participation. Then the sun rises, traffic gathers, and the page fills. Still, some quiet margin remains, waiting to be noticed.",
